winewayland: a non-topmost click-through owned window stacks with its owner

Lightroom Classic dims its main window behind the Adobe sign-in (and behind What's New) with a
popup sized to its client area: WS_POPUP, WS_EX_LAYERED|WS_EX_TRANSPARENT|WS_EX_TOOLWINDOW, owned
by AgWinMainFrame, unmanaged. neutron_is_clickthrough_overlay() also required WS_EX_NOACTIVATE,
so this window missed it and became an xdg_popup, which KWin keeps above everything: the sign-in
window and every other application on that screen were dimmed until sign-in (GitHub issue
Neutron#5). On Windows an owned window that is not topmost sits directly above its owner and
below anything raised over the owner, which is exactly a subsurface. NOACTIVATE is now required
only for topmost windows; menus and tooltips are topmost and keep the popup path.

Verified in the clean room with NEUTRON_POPUP_TRACE=1, Lightroom Classic 15.6.0, same prefix and
runtime: before, the overlay is XDG_POPUP and the sign-in card measures 66/255; after, it is a
SUBSURFACE, the sign-in card is 255 (white), and Lightroom's own window is still dimmed exactly
as before (the same pixel values).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/window.c
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/window.c
@@ -930,11 +930,19 @@
  * user clicks another application (verified with a foreground probe), the handler logs
  * DEACTIVATING, and the bezel still does not sink -- because on Windows WS_EX_TOPMOST really is
  * absolute in the z-order, so the application is right to keep it up. Wayland has no such
- * concept, so the only honest mapping is to stack it with its parent. */
+ * concept, so the only honest mapping is to stack it with its parent.
+ *
+ * WS_EX_NOACTIVATE is not required when the window is NOT topmost (2026-10-10, GitHub issue
+ * Neutron#5): Lightroom Classic dims its main window behind the Adobe sign-in with a 2194x1177
+ * popup, layered+transparent+toolwindow, owned by AgWinMainFrame, without NOACTIVATE. As an
+ * xdg_popup it dimmed the sign-in window and every other application. On Windows an owned window
+ * that is not topmost sits directly above its owner and below anything raised over the owner --
+ * exactly a subsurface. Menus and tooltips are topmost, so they keep the popup path. */
 static BOOL neutron_is_clickthrough_overlay(HWND hwnd)
 {
     DWORD ex = NtUserGetWindowLongW(hwnd, GWL_EXSTYLE);
-    return (ex & WS_EX_LAYERED) && (ex & WS_EX_TRANSPARENT) && (ex & WS_EX_NOACTIVATE);
+    return (ex & WS_EX_LAYERED) && (ex & WS_EX_TRANSPARENT) &&
+           ((ex & WS_EX_NOACTIVATE) || !(ex & WS_EX_TOPMOST));
 }
 void WAYLAND_WindowPosChanged(HWND hwnd, HWND insert_after, HWND owner_hint, UINT swp_flags,
                               const struct window_rects *new_rects, struct window_surface *surface)
